--- ready to file at github.com/greensock/gsap-skills, unsent ---

Title: gsap-scrolltrigger: horizontal scroll snippet calls `Max.max`, which
       throws ReferenceError

Body:
`skills/gsap-scrolltrigger/SKILL.md` line 235 reads:

    xPercent: () => Max.max(0, window.innerWidth - scrollingEl.offsetWidth),

`Max` is not a JavaScript global. Anyone copying this snippet gets
`Uncaught ReferenceError: Max is not defined` the moment the tween evaluates
its function value — so the documented horizontal-scroll recipe cannot run as
written. `Math.max` is presumably intended.

While fixing the typo, two nearby things are worth correcting in the same
snippet, since a reader who hits the ReferenceError will be editing these lines
anyway:

1. The travel distance is computed from `window.innerWidth -
   scrollingEl.offsetWidth`. The distance the content actually needs to move is
   its own overflow, `scrollWidth - clientWidth`. Guarding at 0 also stops
   non-overflowing content from moving at all.

2. `end: "+=1000"` is a fixed scroll length that does not match the travel
   distance, so the pin runs long or short depending on content width. Deriving
   `end` from the same distance function gives a 1:1 feel, and
   `invalidateOnRefresh: true` makes both function values recompute on resize.

Suggested:

    const distance = () =>
      Math.max(0, scrollingEl.scrollWidth - document.documentElement.clientWidth);

    const scrollTween = gsap.to(scrollingEl, {
      x: () => -distance(),
      ease: "none",
      scrollTrigger: {
        trigger: scrollingEl,
        pin: scrollingEl.parentNode,
        start: "top top",
        end: () => "+=" + distance(),
        invalidateOnRefresh: true,
      },
    });

Only the `Max.max` typo is a defect; items 1 and 2 are quality suggestions and
can be dropped if you would rather keep the change minimal.
